# models/coder1b/config.nsl
# NSL-Coder-1B Configuration
#
# Llama-3.2-1B-style shape with our 49152-token vocab. ~1.07B params.
# Sized to be the scale where FASE Deferred is load-bearing: tape AD OOMs
# on 16 GB VRAM, source-AD + FASE fits.

# Architecture
const VOCAB_SIZE = 49152
const D_MODEL = 2048
const N_LAYERS = 16
const N_HEADS = 32
const N_KV_HEADS = 8
const HEAD_DIM = 64
const D_FF = 8192
const MAX_SEQ_LEN = 2048
const ROPE_THETA = 500000.0
const DROPOUT = 0.0

# Pretrain hyperparameters — MUST agree with pretrain_prod.nsl; enforced by
# crates/nsl-cli/tests/pretrain_prod_agreement_gate.rs, which checks all THREE
# model sizes. The scheduler numbers are DERIVED from the real corpus:
#   PRETRAIN_TOTAL_STEPS = PRETRAIN_EPOCHS
#                        * floor(TRAIN_TOKENS / (MAX_SEQ_LEN * PRETRAIN_BATCH_SIZE))
#
# (Roadmap item 10. The previous 100000/2000 pair described a corpus this repo
# has never contained — the same fiction item 4 removed at 50M and item 9 at
# 500M. `PRETRAIN_GRAD_ACCUM` was renamed to the 50M/500M spelling
# `PRETRAIN_GRAD_ACCUMULATION` so ONE gate covers every size; nothing imported
# the old name.)
#
# The schedule deliberately MATCHES the 500M recipe where it can: 16,384
# tokens per optimizer step, 512 optimizer steps, the same corpus slice and the
# same held-out tail. batch 2 x seq 2048 = 4096 tokens per micro-step is four
# times the 500M micro-step, hence grad_accumulation 4 rather than 8.
# That makes the two runs comparable, NOT controlled: they also differ in
# DROPOUT (0.1 at 500M, 0.0 here) and ROPE_THETA (10000 vs 500000), and
# dropout is exactly the knob that moves a train-to-held-out gap. Do not read
# a VAL_LOSS difference between the sizes as a size effect.
#
# CORPUS V2 (item 6, 2026-08-25): this recipe now trains on the 22.6B-token
# v2 mixture (60% Stack code / 39.8% FineWeb / 0.2% NSL;
# models/datasets/CORPUS_MANIFEST_v2.json is its committed identity, incl.
# benchmark decontamination). 22.6B tokens against ~1.07B params is
# ~21 tokens/param — Chinchilla-ish, the first genuinely-sized budget any
# recipe in this repo has carried. Validation is the v2 held-out pair:
# stack_val (repository-disjoint by verified repo_id intersection) and
# web_val (parquet-file-disjoint), both scanned clean against
# HumanEval/MBPP. The old shared prod_train/prod_val slices (v1 tokenizer,
# 8.39M tokens) remain the 50M/500M recipes' corpus; the two generations
# are not loss-comparable — different tokenizer, different text.
const TRAIN_TOKENS = 22595922912
const STACK_VAL_TOKENS = 15377827
const WEB_VAL_TOKENS = 11942304
# LR AND WARMUP — MEASURED AT 1B by a matched-pair comparison (item 6).
#
# This config carried lr = 3e-4, the same constant coder50m / coder500m /
# coder7b all carry across d_model 512 / 1280 / 2048 / 4096. That uniformity IS
# the defect: the value was never checked at any size until item 9 found it too
# high at 500M, where halving it was worth 0.57 nats of held-out loss.
#
# Item 10 tried to repeat that comparison at 1B rather than assume a 500M result
# transfers across a 1.6x width change. It ran two full epochs — 3e-4 and
# 1.5e-4 — and THE ARMS DID NOT SEPARATE INTO AN ANSWER:
#   - neither descends. armA's last-half train mean (9.408) is WORSE than its
#     first half (9.166); armB's is flat (9.162 -> 9.101).
#   - both land just under the ln(49152) = 10.803 uniform-predictor bound:
#     VAL 10.284 at 3e-4, 10.525 at 1.5e-4.
#   - the ordering FLIPS with the horizon (1.5e-4 leads early, trails on
#     held-out), one seed each, and no null control was ever obtained.
#   - worse: 1.5e-4's held-out number is driven by a late excursion that DID
#     NOT REPRODUCE when the same run was resumed from its own step-1200
#     checkpoint. The comparison rests on a value its own rerun contradicts.
# So the sign of "which LR is better at 1B" is not established, and this file
# must not launder either arm into a measured constant.
#
# Third revision, each one measured (the provenance chain matters here):
#   1e-4  (width-scaled default)  — ZERO post-warmup slope at pilot budget
#         (PILOT1B_VALIDATION_2026_08_25.md).
#   3e-5  (pilot matched pair)    — beat 1e-4 by 1.6-1.9 nats on identical
#         batches; BUT the pilot's short cosine decayed lr below ~2.5e-5
#         during its best descent, so 3e-5-HELD was never actually tested.
#         The intermediate run tested it: THREE independent legs (bf16 and
#         f32, different starting θ, stationarily-shuffled data with
#         verified-normal statistics) all REVERSED upward once warmup ended
#         and lr sat at 3e-5, and held-out DEGRADED a full nat by 229M
#         tokens on the bf16 leg (bf16's standing rounding bias amplified
#         the rise — see POSTURE_1B_THROUGHPUT_2026_08_27.md — but the f32
#         legs rose too).
#   1.5e-5 (this value)           — the trajectory-drift probe: the same
#         opt-2000 state, identical loader/RNG batches, halved-peak
#         schedule, descended monotonically through the exact region where
#         3e-5 reversed (paired delta -0.97 by micro 17k and growing).
# min_lr keeps the arms' 0.3 peak ratio (1.5e-5 * 0.3 = 4.5e-6).
const PRETRAIN_LR = 0.000015
# Warmup 8192 micro-steps = 2,048 optimizer steps = ~33.5M tokens (~0.15% of
# the schedule). A CHOSEN default in the large-corpus convention, not a
# measurement — the item-6 pilot validated the shape (descent through
# warmup in both arms; the ramp is not the problem). The old 200 was
# ~9.8% of a 2048-micro toy schedule; carrying that fraction to 22.6B would
# warm up for 2.2B tokens.
const PRETRAIN_WARMUP = 8192
# floor(22,595,922,912 / (2048 * 2)) = 5,516,582 micro-steps (1 epoch).
const PRETRAIN_TOTAL_STEPS = 5516582
const PRETRAIN_MIN_LR = 0.0000045
const PRETRAIN_BATCH_SIZE = 2
const PRETRAIN_GRAD_ACCUMULATION = 4
const PRETRAIN_WEIGHT_DECAY = 0.1
const PRETRAIN_BETA1 = 0.9
const PRETRAIN_BETA2 = 0.95
const PRETRAIN_EPOCHS = 1
const PRETRAIN_GRAD_CLIP = 1.0
# 2,000 optimizer steps ≈ 83 min at the measured selective+bf16 6,568 tok/s — a
# 12.9 GB θ+sidecar rewrite per cadence. 100 was ~9 min at this scale.
const PRETRAIN_CHECKPOINT_EVERY = 2000
# Shuffled, for the reason the 500M recipe records: a concatenated corpus
# should not be fed in file order, and item 8 made a seeded shuffle
# reproducible and resumable (it was entropy-keyed before). NOT measured to
# improve quality at 500M.
const PRETRAIN_SHUFFLE = 1

# Inference
const TOP_K = 40
const TEMPERATURE = 0.8
const MAX_GEN_TOKENS = 512

# Paths (relative to this directory). Item 6: the 1B trains on the v2
# mixture and scores on its two SEPARATE held-out source bins — no
# prefix/tail cut, no make_prod_split. Disjointness is owned by the corpus
# machinery: verify.py's repo_id intersection for stack_val, parquet-file
# granularity for web_val, and the corpus manifest gate's file-list check.
# (The 500M recipe still reads the v1 prod slices; the two generations are
# not loss-comparable.)
const PRETRAIN_DATA = "../../data/tokens/mix/pretrain_train.bin"
const PRETRAIN_VAL_DATA_STACK = "../../data/tokens/mix/stack_val.bin"
const PRETRAIN_VAL_DATA_WEB = "../../data/tokens/mix/web_val.bin"
const CHECKPOINT_DIR = "checkpoints"

# Parameter budget (approximate, f32):
#   Embedding (tied LM head): 49152 × 2048 =   100.66M
#   Per TransformerBlock:
#     GQA Q:                  2048 × 2048 =     4.19M
#     GQA K:                  2048 ×  512 =     1.05M
#     GQA V:                  2048 ×  512 =     1.05M
#     GQA O:                  2048 × 2048 =     4.19M
#     SwiGLU (gate+up+down):  3 × (2048×8192) = 50.33M
#     Block total:                          ≈   60.81M
#   16 blocks:                              ≈    972.9M
#   Final RMSNorm:                          ≈         0
#   TOTAL:                                  ≈   1074M  (~1.07B)
